How the menu route to a bookmarked server was found, rather than guessed.

The order was PREDICTED FIRST from the source, so that the screenshots were a
check and not a search:

  net_game() (src/tron/gGame.cpp:2397) constructs cust, mas, fav, bud, opt,
  lan, inter -- and uMenu renders the highest index at the top and opens on
  its last-constructed item, so the screen reads Online Multiplayer, LAN
  Multiplayer, Network Setup, Mates, Server Bookmarks, Subcultures, Custom
  Connect, Exit Menu, with the cursor on the first of those.

  gServerFavoritesHolder::AddFavorite (src/tron/gServerFavorites.cpp:149)
  scans slots NUM_FAVORITES-1 .. 0 and takes the FIRST EMPTY one, so on a
  fresh profile CUSTOM_SERVER_NAME lands in slot 9, not slot 0. And
  sg_GenerateConnectionItems walks 9 .. 0, so slot 9's item is constructed
  last -- which is the item uMenu opens on.

Then this script was run once against the same build, shooting every level:

  drive-browser.mjs --headed --script-file discover.steps

and the shots confirmed the prediction exactly:

  05-network-menu-top.png              Online Multiplayer selected
  06-down1.png                         LAN Multiplayer
  07-down2.png                         Network Setup
  08-down3.png                         Mates
  09-down4-expect-bookmarks.png        Server Bookmarks       <- 4 Downs
  10-bookmarks-menu-expect-127-0-0-1   "Connect to 127.0.0.1" ALREADY selected

So from the top of the network menu it is Enter, Enter, Down x4, Enter, Enter.
web/tools/bridge-gate.steps uses Down x3 because it arrives with LAN
Multiplayer selected, having entered LAN Multiplayer earlier in the same run;
that is recorded in the steps file next to the count so the two numbers are
not read as a contradiction.

WHY NOT CUSTOM CONNECT, which is the menu that looks made for this: its
address field is a uMenuItemString, so using it means TYPING an address, and
the driver's vocabulary is key:NAME with no text-entry step. The bookmark
route needs only Enter and Down.
